[{"id":"VRCE-2-69-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":1,"english":"As the high-souled Bharata was thus wailing, Kausalyā recognizing his voice spoke to Sumitra.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.85,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a5b0399fd9b644e83b7a2de7f221ecdc2302dc4908c665a5aa49e3a536f05232","compact":"75fc053629fbe97a733680903fef5247d4474977c663b0f29d36019ba7ffd67a","nasal":"75fc053629fbe97a733680903fef5247d4474977c663b0f29d36019ba7ffd67a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":2,"english":"Come is Bharata, the son of that one of crooked ways. I am desirous of seeing the farsighted Bharata.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"91f0dab45f8f90287ad4a03f2d499fd1c876183457249fab2ba382aac2e15ff6","compact":"421ae1755845ddb25cac62021e3e05c2df07a8279028cf948a2b5b51f902d3b7","nasal":"421ae1755845ddb25cac62021e3e05c2df07a8279028cf948a2b5b51f902d3b7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":3,"english":"Having said this to Sumitrā, that one emaciated and with a pallid countenance, trembling and almost deprived of sensation, went to where Bharata, was. And it came to pass that the king's son, Bharata, along with Śatrughna had just then by the way that led to the same, been proceeding to Kausalyā's quarter.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.705,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f4de7377a87aa50d81370f277039504eaa3b7e66b575c2e1447889a9b4ee7f3f","compact":"d436a9de643ccfa29984c05f0418b26f1253d51d7c39d3ab43eb99c2cf1ad8b5","nasal":"324cdf401716e65ef35a47bf1c4f152b64d8a47ecc40e5c7fc841aca0355b877"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":4,"english":"Having said this to Sumitrā, that one emaciated and with a pallid countenance, trembling and almost deprived of sensation, went to where Bharata, was. And it came to pass that the king's son, Bharata, along with Śatrughna had just then by the way that led to the same, been proceeding to Kausalyā's quarter.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.847,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7a6bb396a2757bbd16bfe416d5755351e4da1157d39096ec9cddf76f2361f520","compact":"03381ad91db48c7ae6af8bded0c4bb4eec4c98d6e8a3d043986e822ad6b6b1e3","nasal":"03381ad91db48c7ae6af8bded0c4bb4eec4c98d6e8a3d043986e822ad6b6b1e3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":5,"english":"Then Śatrughna and Bharata seeing the aggrieved Kausalyā, embraced the stricken and fallen lady wellnigh deprived of her senses. Thereupon, weeping from grief, the noble and intelligent Kausalyà exceedingly afflicted, embracing them as they indulged in sorrow, said to Bharata.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.761,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"34ae458723a953a21f726614ade4eb29320bad05ef8f62ec09e134038d6208d2","compact":"4d793d31083333eff3a291e6ee7da3d12db9f037a8389b8bb03a3908a49c0dfb","nasal":"4d793d31083333eff3a291e6ee7da3d12db9f037a8389b8bb03a3908a49c0dfb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":6,"english":"Then Śatrughna and Bharata seeing the aggrieved Kausalyā, embraced the stricken and fallen lady wellnigh deprived of her senses. Thereupon, weeping from grief, the noble and intelligent Kausalyà exceedingly afflicted, embracing them as they indulged in sorrow, said to Bharata. Thus have you, that had desired the kingdom, received it rid of its thorn. Alas! it has been speedily obtained through the crooked act of Kaikeyi.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.592,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-6","DUTT-2-75-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9011c9d56b74412513981b5c35af67254c43352a23c7ea84a70a6dd227785971","compact":"07e1f828af26a0bf1d6a03e23b2903bf2fb9aa1a04609ce1de8675ecf476f365","nasal":"9c1229600dfb1573df1c4bbdbde1bd2c8a5361dda7186ecb9f6b699f703f95c2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":7,"english":"What is the good that is perceived by Kaikeyi of tortuous sight in sending away my son clad in bark to the woods?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fc7ef33ee312041d5c79b028d261aca86b0a10b82560cc4773ed05bd06771de8","compact":"4779385735278cb83df9d14a7a2ee0e6808256572b2cd3de45da31ab7d0dc78c","nasal":"4779385735278cb83df9d14a7a2ee0e6808256572b2cd3de45da31ab7d0dc78c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":8,"english":"It behoves Kaikeyi to send me also speedily to where is staying my illustrious son furnished with a gold-gleaming navel.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d56f26d9eb380ad8e8c7f2a5b01c71184eddbf7ed2fdf92d6dddf22c2a15a96f","compact":"3e216c60d1908354526bdfd8c5b9aa057f141c7c4219d7a9d60df2987dc0cb6e","nasal":"3e216c60d1908354526bdfd8c5b9aa057f141c7c4219d7a9d60df2987dc0cb6e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":9,"english":"Or first performing the fire-sacrifice, I followed by Sumitrā, will myself happily seek the way by which Rāghava (has gone to the woods).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c66207eb98b36f950db604280a67ec8eff069957f7183a9c183764c644a702b7","compact":"11c83313e192a1a31f5f71989a3baccd180c2fb6469f1b66366f00c0724e1ce3","nasal":"11c83313e192a1a31f5f71989a3baccd180c2fb6469f1b66366f00c0724e1ce3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":10,"english":"Or it behoves you to yourself bring me to the place where that foremost of men, my son, is performing austerities.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.889,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc6b2660dfbc3bfc7e31604907a77f7c87b4e88bb572ba97e21d6ce13f38b24c","compact":"963962ab664ccb106920a9fb8a47eeb9d3fd3d153b4807738059eb7bdd49c362","nasal":"963962ab664ccb106920a9fb8a47eeb9d3fd3d153b4807738059eb7bdd49c362"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":11,"english":"This spacious kingdom abounding in corn and wealth, and filled with elephants, horses, and cars, is yours, having been conferred on you (by Kaikeyi).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.778,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"11553c69fd42c27d45f62052380a919e50f5fe9183efc2ba86210927148685dd","compact":"18bc4e78416f4f57b26de7bca4ae9e8fa5f5f4699f4f571954a1efa7079a60e0","nasal":"18bc4e78416f4f57b26de7bca4ae9e8fa5f5f4699f4f571954a1efa7079a60e0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":12,"english":"Exceedingly agitated, he fell at Kausalya's feet, lamenting much, and well nigh deprived of his Bharata then regained his consciousness, and with joined hands answered Kausalya lamenting thus and overcome with excess of sorrow, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.606,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ab80aab0bc34779cb713f36663778da3806bf4f9aff22a5b1ecde5321e128c68","compact":"a71f67db93e85db2aecdc7afabc528e5401b7d42517a7af9d4eb879e69abc06e","nasal":"7cf4fee22e6b08bf104c22334441bba71aedbe6a20d01480f7fa34cf35f61205"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":13,"english":"O noble lady, what for do you censure me who am without sin and who know nothing of this? You know that profuse is my delight in Rāghava.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.609,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9e693ee776dd46d88c331b46e828ae1164a20612aa1b1bf6f2e14b660583bc10","compact":"855267ec38175fff107fb79d34b0c1ab6dc06861fee6f5c6bf7811a3aecedcc6","nasal":"9bfb6436338f0427681e7e357d165488c4c54f693b28c71514e01303d215a400"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":14,"english":"May the sense of him never follow scripture, that had approved the exile into the woods of that foremost of the good, the noble Rāma intent senses. on truth!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.942,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"77b13b828b9fb0584531eee98b30541cc85cae78261eeb9137887436bf94898b","compact":"406490dc1a265335eb4509781e1fe6000d651b56fe56a6bbbe6a892d761bf6be","nasal":"406490dc1a265335eb4509781e1fe6000d651b56fe56a6bbbe6a892d761bf6be"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":15,"english":"May such a person as had approved the exile to the woods of the noble one undergo servitude at the hands of the sinful, answer the calls of nature facing the Sun, and kick a sleeping cow with his feet!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.716,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e4a7a7afbd90cde76146caf2709695446bfc823eb672713063beca61c37b2e11","compact":"d37257a199416e52cb0c52cce4e12c618921c54ba6510ec96d93b8791408afef","nasal":"716fc1d9b8d73c19d5b9d43978549cb682aac90ebcce54d77d1ee7a123855c27"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":16,"english":"May he that had approved the exile of the noble one into the woods come by the sin that is reaped by a master who withholds salary from servants after the ceremony is over! May the person that had approved the exile of the noble one to the woods, reap the sin of his that having taken a sixth pare of their incomes, does not protect his subjects!","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-18","DUTT-2-75-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"61fd3d8d81bd1bbabd8805a92678d96006a5fced51ced4e3a3fac26941d90a57","compact":"2ffb0381b439ac23dcbf04a4b9f0078f6cfbf09b17d02f9dc5176f3254fce35b","nasal":"2ffb0381b439ac23dcbf04a4b9f0078f6cfbf09b17d02f9dc5176f3254fce35b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":17,"english":"May that one that had approved the exile of the noble Rāma to the woods, be guilty of the sin that is his that injures a sovereign engaged in ruling his subjects like sons! May he that had approved the exile of the noble one to the woods, come by the demerit that is his that denies Dakșiņās to ascetics in a sacrifice, after having promised them the same!","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-19","DUTT-2-75-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8365c69076989c3306d05b3590ffa56a17f76662e25792d026190414e3f8eab4","compact":"2df4219953df0d0cb0e14a9a0661eb65716d23d1fdf388c03ac574eb41b97417","nasal":"2df4219953df0d0cb0e14a9a0661eb65716d23d1fdf388c03ac574eb41b97417"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":18,"english":"May he that had approved the exile of the noble one into the woods come by the sin that is reaped by a master who withholds salary from servants after the ceremony is over! May the person that had approved the exile of the noble one to the woods, reap the sin of his that having taken a sixth pare of their incomes, does not protect his subjects!","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.765,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-18","DUTT-2-75-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2c71bb5deab8b005b4e1421915885ae7dcc317d040ab0e83cc9f73c92479d05e","compact":"deaea1332d55c74eaeab528f782161e00791a42d5aec9d63b5c60b4a72fecba6","nasal":"deaea1332d55c74eaeab528f782161e00791a42d5aec9d63b5c60b4a72fecba6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":19,"english":"May that one that had approved the exile of the noble Rāma to the woods, be guilty of the sin that is his that injures a sovereign engaged in ruling his subjects like sons! May he that had approved the exile of the noble one to the woods, come by the demerit that is his that denies Dakșiņās to ascetics in a sacrifice, after having promised them the same!","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.791,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-19","DUTT-2-75-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2a12381cdbf6c305da7e3db43fec469f3aaec949ec1ce18c7a92976cff884f3e","compact":"6555ae241199e862aff697e028cd3f871f38b592a45f60b002b0fcd4104a93c7","nasal":"6555ae241199e862aff697e028cd3f871f38b592a45f60b002b0fcd4104a93c7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":20,"english":"May he that had approved the exile into the woods of the noble one, never discharge the duty of the good in the field thronged with elephants and horses and cars, and bristling with arms!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.765,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d1e7bc3f4ab9abf73258b8d59e2ec15235cff2f881bcb4978bba71535e53bc9b","compact":"e4822196032aef6a6f8f52b0b066a8b8e68536b400780e38c80ba6a986e9827d","nasal":"e4822196032aef6a6f8f52b0b066a8b8e68536b400780e38c80ba6a986e9827d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":21,"english":"May the wicked wight that had approved the exile of the noble one to the woods bring to naught the subtle scriptural knowledge which has been carefully taught him by his intelligent preceptor!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b3b2b12de0e58b687c031787e7980cf8c4c96550a8b90c10dae99519882f11fd","compact":"7d97a152a7d54de18fe6d0231acb6bd4e0888b22d8d8e443297094133364be9a","nasal":"7d97a152a7d54de18fe6d0231acb6bd4e0888b22d8d8e443297094133364be9a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":22,"english":"May that one void of aversion, that had approved of the exile of the noble one to the woods feed on Payasa, Krsara, and goat's flesh, which have not been offered in sacrifice; and may he insult his spiritual precept.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.73,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6dc41b73771da7ea862e1fd5544ce916f3b365297e309b9cfa7cc3a3feec1c6d","compact":"7a18de146302c295c771b10d4c075e46d9ca9c132e14da8667208075f680d447","nasal":"7a18de146302c295c771b10d4c075e46d9ca9c132e14da8667208075f680d447"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":23,"english":"May he that had approved of the exile into the woods of the noble one, eat alone in his home, although surrounded by sons and wives and servitors!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"31ec49935676f0f3ec428d1daa9d549c14295a8833b72381dcb341867c3579e7","compact":"e3dbc8df2709cf84165bcdb613871ca26321ead794c912ec79cf64c30d469d77","nasal":"e3dbc8df2709cf84165bcdb613871ca26321ead794c912ec79cf64c30d469d77"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":24,"english":"And may he come by the sin that is reaped by slaying a king, a woman, a boy, or an old man, or by renouncing those who have claims on his maintenance!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5555489f84151025e44eff1d6fff61cf07a0619e9976b64114aac6576d33121d","compact":"aeaccd913bde676a21ce34f6909a4983dd591c09637239db109ae7614ef72eda","nasal":"aeaccd913bde676a21ce34f6909a4983dd591c09637239db109ae7614ef72eda"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":25,"english":"May he that had approved of the exile into the woods of that noble one, come by the demerit that is his who sleeps during both the twilight's! May he reap the sin that is reaped by an incendiary or him that Leith with his preceptor's wife; or him that wrongs his friend!","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-39"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"11d2b6f498b72ad18630cad994f6721e6d98829f923cb8f50e1f528a6c6b2b43","compact":"2973cf612fd16962621058bf65cfb5faf8abc59a7bd491799c5ee40c33ad1967","nasal":"2973cf612fd16962621058bf65cfb5faf8abc59a7bd491799c5ee40c33ad1967"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":26,"english":"May he that had approved of the exile into the woods of that noble one, come by the demerit that is his who sleeps during both the twilight's! May he reap the sin that is reaped by an incendiary or him that Leith with his preceptor's wife; or him that wrongs his friend!","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-39"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8aa46f4a938acefa8c8b9bb5434b9c7b4922dfdfc1e24fb1b554689363fdc028","compact":"15cb44ababe910fdf4a8b8a77580695fa1e493c244c04373409e556624297271","nasal":"15cb44ababe910fdf4a8b8a77580695fa1e493c244c04373409e556624297271"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-27","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":27,"english":"May he that had approved the exile of that noble one into the woods, never serve the gods or the manes of the ancestors or his father and mother!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-40"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069027","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1c47fd4a83cb5d99b1a6e3ee990e92b1235e16c5bbe9ed7419e933e00f50241d","compact":"139f4abe43da5f21db21a53d425de7a4011f64ddcf3731a57c9e844013beabd6","nasal":"139f4abe43da5f21db21a53d425de7a4011f64ddcf3731a57c9e844013beabd6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-28","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":28,"english":"may he that had approved the exile of that noble one into the woods, be speedily shut out from the world of the good, from their fame, and from their acts!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-41"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069028","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cb37305d1eae3a547e2fd73d0981654c218ed4c9b417d3c709c321d429fdbc5e","compact":"562c902405fee96b78bcc273633ca4163929fd2830bfcd05f1764be9a848da32","nasal":"562c902405fee96b78bcc273633ca4163929fd2830bfcd05f1764be9a848da32"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-29","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":29,"english":"Having thus consoled Kausalyā bereft of her son and husband, the prince afflicted with distress fell down.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.569,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-53"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069029","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d3700cba96e1ab5563553fb84c009930f06e1fad87624dddf7bed0f3982735b5","compact":"d395f3c2941a041b5845433aeb2a25d92c6b7a309c23688f1836251105f857a1","nasal":"2cecd66e432997ae978699322b8331706a81565e6f15eacecddc01794bfde93e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-30","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":30,"english":"Then Kausalyā addressed the aggrieved Bharaia (well-nigh) deprived of his senses, swearing strong oaths, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.909,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-54"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069030","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3615b6c79edb75ef03f784386aa39268be8d4649a1c08d00d4b666f53f6073a2","compact":"f602ef0453a5210a2f15da6a15836f460e03b4e70da4833882cead4a88899350","nasal":"17b00d89e46c9ccb11fa1804f99326879abf48e7f7eb6b579eb00059347bafe0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-31","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":31,"english":"O son, it grieves me more that you are afflicting my heart by taking oaths.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-55"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069031","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bd3f5628fc37f08ace214283e69d26f2ec262cc2e57eef6d34504fc7973cd56f","compact":"86cb543d289db2ae8c0cdb2c734a76bcd3606b2e27d46f3a3454cab87846e974","nasal":"86cb543d289db2ae8c0cdb2c734a76bcd3606b2e27d46f3a3454cab87846e974"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-32","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":32,"english":"Lucky it is that graced with auspicious marks, you swerves not from virtue. My child, for this reason you will attain the regions of pious person, drawing into her lap Bharata attached to his brother, and embracing the mighty armed one, gave way to grief.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.731,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-56"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069032","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6dd5ab20e7851bd11a9a6ff10bab01855225e1bab2b3ed31d71fdcb20ef91a42","compact":"b0d18e4cb98e0a1976e5541c5a8416271524263e276bd3add443d91c89f253f0","nasal":"b0d18e4cb98e0a1976e5541c5a8416271524263e276bd3add443d91c89f253f0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-33","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":33,"english":"And bewailing thus, the mind of the high souled (Bharata) wrought with sorrow, was overcome with the burden of grief.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.839,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-57"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069033","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ec4e9e039baae79f51220ea6322fa1464f1084a2b29cc81bc6040b2b43362024","compact":"ace14f81f146ad3f2edaaeaa9ea4da2c429b3738b3475dc536c44e2beb4b98fb","nasal":"ace14f81f146ad3f2edaaeaa9ea4da2c429b3738b3475dc536c44e2beb4b98fb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-69-34","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":69,"verse":34,"english":"And fallen the ground, lamenting, senseless, with his intellect overpowered, and momentarily heaving sighs, Bharata passed away night in grief.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.722,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-58"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2069034","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fb9e9735fa840b473374f23742187d33657a31f307a8ef18cd5fc5c49580ff03","compact":"161f0eaa93dee5d3d98f56308e42dc6c0065c8b2e8d919cc49d35ed92fe54899","nasal":"161f0eaa93dee5d3d98f56308e42dc6c0065c8b2e8d919cc49d35ed92fe54899"}}]
